\title{bibnets: Importing, Constructing, and Exporting Bibliometric Networks}
\description{
\code{bibnets} builds bibliometric networks from the exports of scholarly
databases. A bibliometric network links authors, documents, references,
keywords, journals, institutions or countries through the records they
share: co-authorship links authors who write together, co-citation links
references cited together (Small, 1973), and bibliographic coupling links
documents that cite the same work (Kessler, 1963). These networks show how
a research field is organized: its collaborating groups, its intellectual
base and its topics. The weight of a link can be normalized for how active
each entity is (\code{\link[=normalize]{normalize()}}), and authorship credit can be counted in
full, fractionally or by author position (the \code{counting} argument of the
builders). Networks can
be built for time windows (\code{\link[=temporal_network]{temporal_network()}}), reduced to their backbone
with the disparity filter (\code{\link[=backbone]{backbone()}}; Serrano et al., 2009), and traced
over time with a historiograph of the most locally cited documents
(\code{\link[=historiograph]{historiograph()}}; Garfield, 2004).

Records are read with \code{\link[=read_biblio]{read_biblio()}} from Scopus, Web of Science,
OpenAlex, BibTeX, RIS, Lens.org, Dimensions and Crossref exports, or taken
from any data frame with its own column names and separators. The builders
(\code{\link[=author_network]{author_network()}}, \code{\link[=document_network]{document_network()}}, \code{\link[=reference_network]{reference_network()}},
\code{\link[=keyword_network]{keyword_network()}}, \code{\link[=source_network]{source_network()}}, \code{\link[=institution_network]{institution_network()}},
\code{\link[=country_network]{country_network()}} and the generic \code{\link[=conetwork]{conetwork()}}) return an edge list
with \code{from}, \code{to}, \code{weight} and \code{count} columns, which \code{\link[=to_igraph]{to_igraph()}},
\code{\link[=to_tbl_graph]{to_tbl_graph()}}, \code{\link[=to_cograph]{to_cograph()}}, \code{\link[=to_gephi]{to_gephi()}}, \code{\link[=to_graphml]{to_graphml()}} and
\code{\link[=to_matrix]{to_matrix()}} export.
}
